dialogue trust_cleric_5
title: The first kept name
trigger: Trust cleric level=5
once
speaker commander $commander
speaker hero $with

node open commander
  The Cleric's hands are still after today's battle, for once. By the fire the book of names lies open to a fresh page, a pen balanced across it, nothing written yet.
  * You walked away from the tables. I said that was what counted. I still say it. -> forgiven_kept
    if: FlagSet cleric:white_forgiven
    do: AddTrust $with 5
  * You told me something drove you out. I think something's been driving you on since, too. -> asked_kept
    if: FlagSet cleric:white_asked
    do: AddTrust $with 5
  * I asked you for everything you knew about the collectors. You gave it. Every time. -> used_kept
    if: FlagSet cleric:white_used
    do: AddTrust $with 6
  * Your hands aren't shaking. -> still
    do: AddTrust $with 3

node forgiven_kept hero
  I've had a long while to do the sums since you said it. You were right. One walk, and everything after it. That's the part that counts.
  -> page

node asked_kept hero
  Yes. Her, and then all of you. It turns out a healer can run toward something as well as away. I didn't know that.
  -> page

node used_kept hero
  I did. And every time it kept one of you standing, it was worth a little less of what it cost me to learn. It's nearly paid off now.
  -> page

node still hero
  No. Not after. Not today. I keep looking at them, waiting.
  -> page

node page hero
  I was going to write a name tonight, Commander. The first in a long while. Then I sat here, and I couldn't think who it should be.
  -> thread

node thread commander
  The fire settles. The old pages, the ones full of names, lie heavy under the Cleric's other hand.
  * Read me the next one, then, before the new page. Like the night in the rain. -> read
    if: FlagSet cleric:ledger_read
    do: AddTrust $with 8
    do: SetFlag cleric:thread_read
  * The Choir took them, not you. That's still true. Is it any easier now? -> absolved
    if: FlagSet cleric:ledger_absolved
    do: AddTrust $with 6
    do: SetFlag cleric:thread_absolved
  * I told you to put it away. I was wrong. You were right to keep it. -> wrong
    if: FlagSet cleric:ledger_shut
    do: AddTrust $with 8
    do: SetFlag cleric:thread_wrong
  * Tell me about the girl. The last one. -> girl
    do: AddTrust $with 6
    do: SetFlag cleric:thread_girl

node read hero
  Mabel Rook. Kept bees, talked to them. Stung every week and never minded. There. That's the last of the old book I'll read aloud. It feels like the right place to stop.
  -> kept

node absolved hero
  Easier, yes. Not lighter. But I can carry it now without it carrying me, which isn't the same, and is better.
  -> kept

node wrong hero
  You weren't wrong. You were being a commander. Someone had to tell me it could cost us. I just needed to prove it wouldn't.
  -> kept

node girl hero
  Her name was Wren. She asked me if the colour went somewhere nice. I said yes. I've wanted to know if that was a lie ever since. Now I'll find out, with you.
  -> kept

node kept hero
  So here's the thing I worked out, just now. The new page isn't for the lost. It's for the kept. Everyone this crew has carried home who should not have made it. And I'd like the first name to be one you choose.
  -> choose

node choose commander
  The Cleric holds out the pen.
  * Write all of them. Every one of us who's still here. -> crew
    do: AddTrust $with 7
    do: SetFlag cleric:kept_crew
  * Write your own name first. You've kept yourself alive too. -> self
    do: AddTrust $with 9
    do: SetFlag cleric:kept_self
  * Leave it blank. Names on a page are how the Choir counts people. -> blank
    do: AddTrust $with -2
    do: SetFlag cleric:kept_blank
  * Write it, then sleep. I'll take the watch tonight, like the first night. -> watch
    if: FlagSet founding:watch_cleric
    do: AddTrust $with 9
    do: SetFlag cleric:kept_crew

node crew hero
  All of us. It'll take the whole page. Good. Let it take the whole book. I'd like to run out of room for once.

node self hero
  Me. I hadn't thought of that. I don't think I ever counted myself as someone who was kept. Hold the lamp closer, Commander. My hand's quite steady.

node blank hero
  Maybe. Or maybe it's only the Choir's way if nobody reads them. I'll leave it blank tonight, for you. But I'll keep the pen out.

node watch hero
  Like the first night. You noticed then, and you're still noticing. There. All of you, and you first. Wake me if anyone so much as sneezes.
